      "id": "6ad220d4304f2eea525455c9",
      "fieldData": {
       "meta-title": "Product Market Fit Survey Builder for Startups | Emergent",
       "meta-description": "Build a product market fit survey from a prompt: the must-have question, a very disappointed score by role and quarter, and Slack alerts.",
       "name": "Build a Product Market Fit Survey and Find Your Must-Have Users",
       "acrm---product-name": "Product Market Fit Survey Builder",
       "description": "Build a product market fit survey that counts who would be very disappointed and splits that score by role and by quarter.",
       "acrm---breadcrumb": "Product Market Fit Survey",
       "explore-cta": "Build My Product Market Fit Survey",
       "acrm---key-feature-sub-heading": "A product market fit survey asks users how they would feel without the product. Emergent builds it, and the app scores every reply.",
       "awb---mockup-data": "<p id=\"\">window.awbMockup = { earlyStageSaas: \"Build a product market fit survey for my shift-scheduling app. Send it only to users who opened the app at least twice in the last 2 weeks. Ask how they would feel if they could no longer use it, with very disappointed, somewhat disappointed, and not disappointed as the answers. Show the share of very disappointed answers against a 40% line, with the reply count beside it.\", roleSegments: \"Create a product market fit survey for my proposal-design tool. Ask each person for their role first: agency owner, freelancer, or in-house designer. Then ask the must-have question with its three answers. Show the overall very disappointed share and the share for each role, with the number of replies behind each one, so I can see which role needs the product most.\", scoreOverTime: \"Build a product market fit survey for my route-planning app that goes to each quarter's new users once and never to the same person twice. Chart the very disappointed share by quarter next to the number of replies. When a quarter closes above 40%, post the score to our #product channel in Slack.\", mobileApps: \"Make a phone survey for my habit-tracking app with one question per screen. Start with the must-have question and its three answers. Then ask for the main benefit from a short list: streak reminders, weekly charts, or shared goals. Count the main benefit answers from the users who picked very disappointed.\" };</p>",
       "awb---how-to-title": "How to Create a Product Market Fit Survey",
       "awb---how-to-description": "Seven steps to create a product market fit survey that scores your must-have users and shows which segment to build for.",
       "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Product Market Fit Survey FAQ\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a product market fit survey?\", \"a\": \"A product market fit survey asks current users how they would feel if they could no longer use the product: very disappointed, somewhat disappointed, or not disappointed. The share who pick very disappointed is the score. In Sean Ellis's benchmarking, companies with strong traction almost always had more than 40% of users give that answer.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I create a product market fit survey?\", \"a\": \"Describe the product and the users you want to reach in a prompt to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder'>AI survey builder</a>. Emergent builds the questions and a dashboard that splits the score by group. Every reply is saved to a database you own, and any question can be changed with another prompt.\"}, {\"q\": \"What product market fit survey questions should I ask after the main one?\", \"a\": \"Ask three: what type of people would most benefit from the product, what main benefit they get from it, and how it could improve for them. Superhuman asked these three right after the must-have question. Use the first answer to decide who to sell to, and the second to decide what to protect.\"}, {\"q\": \"What goes into a product market fit survey template?\", \"a\": \"A product market fit survey template holds five parts: a segment question such as role, the must-have question, and three follow-ups on who gains most, what they value, and what to fix. Leave onboarding and support questions for a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder/customer-feedback'>customer feedback survey</a>. Emergent builds the template from your prompt, and you edit it by prompting again.\"}, {\"q\": \"How many responses does a product market fit survey need?\", \"a\": \"Aim for at least 40. Superhuman's write-up puts the point where results turn directionally correct at around 40 respondents. With 40 replies, each one is worth 2.5 points of the score. Treat a change of one or two replies as noise until the count grows.\"}, {\"q\": \"What score on a product market fit survey means you have fit?\", \"a\": \"A score above 40% very disappointed is the line Sean Ellis drew after benchmarking nearly a hundred startups. Treat it as a guide rather than a pass mark: Superhuman started below it and moved closer by narrowing its focus to the users who would be very disappointed.\"}, {\"q\": \"Which users should I send a product market fit survey to?\", \"a\": \"Send it to people who use the product now and know what it does for them. People who only know the product from ads or a demo have nothing to miss yet, and their views fit a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder/brand-perception'>brand perception survey</a> better. For active users, Sean Ellis recommended those who had used the product at least twice in the previous two weeks, and Superhuman followed that rule.\"}, {\"q\": \"How often should you run product market fit surveys?\", \"a\": \"Run them on a fixed schedule, such as every month or every quarter, and send each round to new users only. Superhuman tracked its score weekly, monthly, and quarterly, and it never surveyed the same user twice, to keep the 40% threshold meaningful.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should I do with somewhat disappointed answers on a product market fit survey?\", \"a\": \"Split them by main benefit. Superhuman filtered its somewhat disappointed group on the benefit its biggest fans named. It paid close attention to those who shared that benefit and set aside the users who did not.\"}, {\"q\": \"How is a product market fit survey different from a satisfaction score?\", \"a\": \"A satisfaction score asks how happy someone was with one experience, while a product market fit survey asks how much they would miss the product if it were gone. Send a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder/customer-satisfaction'>customer satisfaction survey</a> after a support ticket or a delivery, and this one when you need to know whether the product is a must-have. Emergent builds either one from a prompt.\"}]}; </script></div></div>",
       "alpb---prompt": "<p>Build a product market fit survey that collects every answer in a database I own.</p><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbPrompt = {\"default\": \"Build a product market fit survey that collects every answer in a database I own.\", \"chips\": [{\"label\": \"Must-Have Score\", \"add\": \"Add a must-have score that shows the share of users who would be very disappointed.\"}, {\"label\": \"Score by Role\", \"add\": \"Add a role question and show the score for each role.\"}, {\"label\": \"Quarterly Cohorts\", \"add\": \"Add quarterly cohorts so each new user is asked once and the score is charted by quarter.\"}, {\"label\": \"Main Benefit\", \"add\": \"Add a main benefit question and count the answers from the very disappointed group.\"}]}; </script></div></div>",
